from pathlib import Path
import csv,json
out=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
v=json.loads((out/'verified_examples.json').read_text())
read=lambda p:list(csv.DictReader(p.open()))
pct=lambda a,n:f'{100*a/n:.2f}'
choices=[('agnews',8,'0110100001000110'),('airline',1,'1011110000001000'),('airline',14,'0010100000001101'),('20ng',3,'0000011110001010'),('climmig',9,'0100001100010011'),('pheme',7,'1000100010001111')]
lines=['# Single-bit profile contrasts in the saved Qwen codes','', 'Exploratory analysis, 30 October 2026. No models were trained and no Overleaf files were changed.','', '## Design and verification','', 'For every standalone corpus included in the paper, enumerate observed 16-bit profiles differing in exactly one coordinate. Retain pairs with at least 50 documents in each profile, and use a stricter minimum of 100 per profile for the examples below. All other 15 coordinates, including their inactive states, are identical within a pair. These are conditional comparisons of observed document groups, not interventions on documents or effects of switching a bit. Encoders and bit numbering are local to each corpus.','', 'All saved profile-by-label flow tables were checked for identical profile totals across annotations. For the displayed standalone examples, encoded-file hashes and label-file hashes were verified against the saved paper provenance; unique document IDs, full corpus size, selected profile membership and all selected label counts were independently recomputed from document-level inputs. `verified_sources.json` and `provenance.json` record sources and hashes.','', 'The enumeration depends only on code membership and support. Displayed examples are subsequently selected for substantive illustration. AG News bit 08, Airline bit 01, 20 Newsgroups bit 03 and PHEME bit 07 are the pairs maximizing the smaller group size in their respective corpora. Airline bit 14 and Climate Migration bit 09 are selected after inspecting annotation contrasts. This selection is exploratory and not a held-out semantic evaluation.','', '## Available comparisons','', '| Corpus | All documents | Pairs with both n >= 50 | Pairs with both n >= 100 | Unique documents in pairs with both n >= 50 |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for r in read(out/'coverage.csv'):lines.append(f"| {r['corpus']} | {r['N']} | {r['pairs_min50']} | {r['pairs_min100']} | {r['unique_documents_min50']} |")
lines+=['','Pairs can share profiles; pair counts are not independent observations. Unique-document coverage counts each document once.','', '## Exact profile definitions','', '| Corpus | Changed bit | Code at 0 | n0 | Code at 1 | n1 |','|---|---:|---|---:|---|---:|']
for c,k,p in choices:
 q=p[:k]+'1'+p[k+1:];a=v[c][p];b=v[c][q]
 lines.append(f'| {c} | {k:02d} | `{p}` | {a["n"]} | `{q}` | {b["n"]} |')
lines+=['','## Interpretation of the selected comparisons','',
'### AG News: an internal distinction within Sports',
'', 'The largest supported pair has 1,050 and 1,224 documents, differing only in bit 08. Sports accounts for 1,023/1,050 (97.43%) and 1,208/1,224 (98.69%). Thus the editorial label changes little.',
'', 'A seeded random sample of 12 documents per profile was read (seed 1234, IDs sorted numerically before sampling). The inactive group includes baseball, basketball and other sports reports; the active group prominently includes American football. Full-group document-presence counts subsequently corroborate this interpretation: `touchdown(s)` or `quarterback(s)` appears in 17/1,050 (1.62%) versus 560/1,224 (45.75%); `home run(s)` or `inning(s)` appears in 251/1,050 (23.90%) versus 18/1,224 (1.47%). Text is HTML-unescaped and lowercased; matching is case-insensitive with word boundaries. These are post-hoc lexical indicators, not validated sport annotations or estimates of the exact fraction of football articles. Samples and exact counts are archived.',
'', 'Illustrations from the random sample: inactive bit 08, ID 2270, “NL Wrap: Walker Grand Slam Helps Cards Beat Reds Again”; active bit 08, ID 6670, “Jones Leads Cowboys to Stunning Win Over Seahawks”.',
'', 'Other AG News contrasts also change category composition. For bit 06 in context `000111?001000000`, the 110/108-document groups have Sci-Tech shares of 10.00% and 87.96%. Inspection suggests a shift from weather/disaster reporting toward scientific/natural-world coverage, but this pair was selected for its large label contrast and should remain exploratory. It is not needed for the main within-Sports example.',
'', '### Airline: sentiment differences within carrier-specific contexts',
'', 'The largest supported pair differs only in bit 01. Its 654/742 documents are 99.54%/99.87% Southwest; negative sentiment is 136/654 (20.80%) versus 293/742 (39.49%). Restricting exactly to Southwest yields 134/651 (20.58%) versus 293/741 (39.54%).',
'', 'For bit 14 in another context, 1,064/156 documents are 99.81%/100% United. Negative sentiment is 495/1,064 (46.52%) versus 127/156 (81.41%). Within United only, it is 494/1,062 (46.52%) versus 127/156 (81.41%). Customer-service complaint labels account for 136/1,064 (12.78%) versus 80/156 (51.28%) of the full groups. This suggests the contrast may involve complaint content as well as polarity; do not name bit 14 a universal sentiment bit.',
'', 'Across all six observed bit-14 profile pairs with at least 100 documents per profile, negative sentiment is higher on the active side, with differences of 10.30 to 34.89 percentage points. The four eligible bit-01 pairs also all have higher negative-sentiment shares on the active side (17.59 to 22.75 points). These comparisons assess repeated conditional associations in one fitted code, not stability across encoder initializations.',
'', '### 20 Newsgroups: differentiation within computing',
'', 'The largest supported pair differs in bit 03 (754/645 documents). Computing remains prevalent: 742/754 (98.41%) versus 642/645 (99.53%). The `comp.windows.x` newsgroup rises from 248/754 (32.89%) to 414/645 (64.19%), while `comp.graphics` falls from 226/754 (29.97%) to 73/645 (11.32%). This is a finer topic contrast within a largely unchanged coarse topic.',
'', '### Climate Migration: different annotation dimensions move differently',
'', 'For the pair differing in bit 09 (115/166 documents), migration relevance increases from 67/115 (58.26%) to 143/166 (86.14%), while the quantitative-methods share is similar: 67/115 (58.26%) versus 96/166 (57.83%). This distinguishes observed annotation dimensions without implying independent latent concepts.',
'', '### PHEME: rumour status within one event',
'', 'The largest supported pair differs in bit 07 (257/337 documents). Both groups consist entirely of Sydney siege tweets. Rumour prevalence rises from 50/257 (19.46%) to 183/337 (54.30%). This demonstrates a contrast within event identity; it does not independently validate why the text or engagement differs.',
'', '## Rechecking the existing Mixed example',
'', 'P01 and P07 differ only in bit 11; P01 has bit 11 inactive and P07 active. The negative-sentiment shares among Airline documents are 2,611/3,401 (76.77%) and 257/556 (46.22%). Carrier composition also changes, so a source-only comparison does not hold carrier fixed. However, rejoining frozen profile membership to the original labels shows that negative sentiment is lower in P07 within every one of the six carriers:',
'', '| Carrier | P01 Airline n | P01 negative (%) | P07 Airline n | P07 negative (%) |', '|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
